1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,981
Backups at night. Logs cleaned every week. A report every Monday morning.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,087
Nobody wants to do these by hand, and Linux has three tools that do them for you.

3
00:00:10,557 --> 00:00:15,593
In this video: schedule tasks using at, cron, and systemd timer units.

4
00:00:20,113 --> 00:00:22,696
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:27,071
Schedule tasks using at, cron and systemd timer units.

6
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:31,916
We'll schedule one task with each tool, wait a minute, and watch all three run.

7
00:00:40,436 --> 00:00:44,256
Three tools, three jobs. At runs a command once, later.

8
00:00:44,276 --> 00:00:47,817
Cron runs a command again and again, on a schedule.

9
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:53,472
A systemd timer starts a systemd service on a schedule, and it is the modern way.

10
00:00:57,942 --> 00:01:00,022
A cron schedule has five fields:

11
00:01:00,042 --> 00:01:05,207
minute, hour, day of the month, month, and day of the week. A star means every.

12
00:01:05,227 --> 00:01:07,373
So five stars means every minute.

13
00:01:12,843 --> 00:01:17,879
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the at and cron packages and starts

14
00:01:17,899 --> 00:01:20,823
their services. These steps change the system.

15
00:01:30,343 --> 00:01:34,139
First, at. Echo prints the command, the pipe hands it to at,

16
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:36,429
and the time is now plus one minute.

17
00:01:36,449 --> 00:01:40,627
At prints a warning, then the job number and the time it will run.

18
00:01:46,097 --> 00:01:50,450
Atq lists the waiting jobs: the number, the time, the queue, and the user.

19
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:59,423
Second, cron. One line: five stars, then the command.

20
00:01:59,443 --> 00:02:02,961
The pipe hands it to crontab with a dash, which means:

21
00:02:02,981 --> 00:02:05,713
read the new table from the pipe. Careful:

22
00:02:05,733 --> 00:02:08,793
this replaces the whole cron table of the user.

23
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:15,940
Crontab dash L lists it.

24
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:20,184
In daily life you edit with crontab dash E, which opens vim.

25
00:02:20,204 --> 00:02:22,093
This job runs every minute.

26
00:02:28,613 --> 00:02:34,378
Third, a systemd timer, which needs two files. First, the service: what to run.

27
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:38,991
Type oneshot means: run, then finish. ExecStart is the command.

28
00:02:39,011 --> 00:02:42,725
Printf writes the lines, backslash N is a new line,

29
00:02:42,745 --> 00:02:48,070
and sudo tee writes the file into slash E T C slash systemd slash system.

30
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:02,480
Second, the timer: when to run. On calendar, minutely, means every minute.

31
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:07,787
Wanted by timers dot target means: start this timer at boot, once it is enabled.

32
00:03:07,807 --> 00:03:10,839
The timer and the service share a name: hello.

33
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:24,445
Systemctl daemon dash reload makes systemd read the new files.

34
00:03:24,465 --> 00:03:29,214
Then enable dash dash now starts the timer, and starts it at every boot.

35
00:03:33,684 --> 00:03:38,058
List dash timers shows when the timer runs next, and which service it starts.

36
00:03:44,578 --> 00:03:47,694
Now we wait a little more than a minute. Sleep seventy five.

37
00:03:51,164 --> 00:03:57,256
The at job ran, and wrote the time. Cron ran, and wrote the time. The timer ran too.

38
00:03:57,276 --> 00:04:00,385
Three tools, same result. And atq is empty:

39
00:04:00,405 --> 00:04:03,368
an at job runs once, and then it is gone.

40
00:04:12,888 --> 00:04:16,324
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

41
00:04:17,844 --> 00:04:23,897
We reboot. Cron is still scheduled, the timer is still enabled, and it shows its next run.

42
00:04:23,917 --> 00:04:27,339
The at job ran already, so nothing is left to show.

43
00:04:36,859 --> 00:04:38,474
Your turn. Three things.

44
00:04:38,494 --> 00:04:43,718
A cron job for student that runs touch slash T M P slash exam dash cron every

45
00:04:43,738 --> 00:04:47,123
fifteen minutes. An at job that runs in two hours.

46
00:04:47,143 --> 00:04:51,754
And a timer called exam dot timer for the existing exam dot service,

47
00:04:51,774 --> 00:04:56,658
every day at three in the morning, enabled and running. Run the checker.

48
00:05:04,128 --> 00:05:05,878
Try before you open the solution.

49
00:05:11,398 --> 00:05:16,882
Next up: start and stop services and configure services to start automatically at boot.

50
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:20,255
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
